## Why This Is Asked

This question probes lock-free concurrency, memory ordering, and real-world scheduler design under high contention across many cores.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free deques and work-stealing semantics (Chase-Lev style)
- Acquire/release semantics and memory barriers
- ABA avoidance (version stamps, pointer tagging)
- Safe memory reclamation (hazard pointers, epochs)
- Correctness under preemption and high thread counts

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo: Chase-Lev style per-core deque (simplified)
typedef struct {
  void* slots[DEQUE_SIZE];
  _Atomic size_t head; // stealing/read by others
  _Atomic size_t tail; // local producer/consumer index
  // versioning to mitigate ABA
  _Atomic size_t stamp;
} Deque;

void enqueue_bottom(Deque* d, void* v){
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&d->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  d->slots[t & MASK] = v;
  atomic_store_explicit(&d->tail, t+1, memory_order_release);
}

void* dequeue_bottom(Deque* d){
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&d->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t == atomic_load_explicit(&d->head, memory_order_acquire)) return NULL;
  void* v = d->slots[(t-1) & MASK];
  atomic_store_explicit(&d->tail, t-1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return v;
}

void* steal_top(Deque* d){
  size_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&d->head, memory_order_acquire);
  size_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&d->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t <= h) return NULL;
  void* v = d->slots[h & MASK];
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&d->head, &h, h+1, memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed))
    return v;
  return NULL;
}
```

